WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.560
A GPU can hold a model's weights and still run
out of memory halfway through a long conversation.

2
00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.980
The KV cache is the
part that keeps growing.

3
00:00:08.916 --> 00:00:11.806
Attention projects a key
and a value for every token.

4
00:00:11.916 --> 00:00:16.566
The KV cache stores those key and
value tensors in VRAM, so the model never

5
00:00:16.566 --> 00:00:19.156
recomputes them for earlier tokens.

6
00:00:19.912 --> 00:00:23.302
Follow one request
with a 1,000-token prompt.

7
00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:28.762
Prefill processes all 1,000 tokens and
writes 1,000 entries into the cache at once.

8
00:00:29.415 --> 00:00:32.215
Decode then grows it one step at a time.

9
00:00:32.405 --> 00:00:37.825
Each decode step reads every stored
entry and adds one new entry for its token.

10
00:00:37.885 --> 00:00:42.775
After 200 decode steps, the
cache holds 1,200 entries.

11
00:00:42.895 --> 00:00:46.115
The cache grows
linearly with sequence length.

12
00:00:46.826 --> 00:00:50.276
Every request in a
batch has its own cache.

13
00:00:50.306 --> 00:00:55.936
Long contexts and large batches multiply
the total, until the cache can become the

14
00:00:56.036 --> 00:00:57.616
main consumer of VRAM.

15
00:00:57.646 --> 00:01:02.456
That is why memory planning reserves
headroom for it, as Chapter 3 shows.

16
00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:05.053
Pause and calculate.

17
00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:07.763
A request arrives with
a 2,500-token prompt.

18
00:01:07.763 --> 00:01:09.153
How many entries does its KV cache

19
00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:11.433
hold after prefill?

20
00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:13.993
How many after 300 decode steps?
